n\nL58: \nL59:   * 【14†orcid.org/0000-0002-8651-869X†orcid.org】\nL60: \nL61: Institute of Information System Engineering, Information Engineering University, Zhengzhou, China\nL62: National Digital Switching System Engineering and Technology Research Center, Zhengzhou, China\nL63: \nL64: 【13†Search for more papers by this author】\nL65: \nL66: 【15†Nae Zheng】,\nL67: \nL68: Nae Zheng\nL69: \nL70: Institute of Information System Engineering, Information Engineering University, Zhengzhou, China\nL71: \nL72: 【15†Search for more papers by this author】\nL73: \nL74: 【7†Zeyu Wang】,\nL75: \nL76: Zeyu Wang\nL77: \nL78:   * 【8†orcid.org/0009-0006-4702-4714†orcid.org】\nL79: \nL80: Institute of Information System Engineering, Information Engineering University, Zhengzhou, China\nL81: 【7†Search for more papers by this author】\nL82: \nL83: 【9†Ding Wang】,\nL84: \nL85: Corresponding Author\nL86: \nL87: Ding Wang\nL88: \nL89:   * 【16†[email protected]】\nL90: \nL91:   * 【11†orcid.org/0000-0001-6533-9206†orcid.org】\nL92: \nL93: Institute of Information System Engineering, Information Engineering University, Zhengzhou, China\nL94: \nL95: National Digital Switching System Engineering and Technology Research Center, Zhengzhou, China\nL96: \nL97: Correspondence: Ding Wang\nL98: \nL99: (【17†[email protected]】)\nL100: \nL101: 【9†Search for more papers by this author】\nL102: \nL103: 【13†Jiexin Yin】,\nL104: \nL105: Jiexin Yin\nL106:   * 【14†orcid.org/0000-0002-8651-869X†orcid.org】\nL107: \nL108: Institute of Information System Engineering, Information Engineering University, Zhengzhou, China\nL109: \nL110: National Digital Switching System Engineering and Technology Research Center, Zhengzhou, China\nL111: \nL112: 【13†Search for more papers by this author】\nL113: \nL114: 【15†Nae Zheng】,\nL115: \nL116: Nae Zheng\nL117: \nL118: Institute of Information System Engineering, Information Engineering University, Zhengzhou, China\nL119: \nL120: 【15†Search for more papers by this author】\nL121: \nL122: First published: 19 January 2026\nL123: 【18†https://doi.org/10.1049/rsn2.70114Digital Object Identifier (DOI)†doi.org】\nL124: \nL125: view metrics\nL126: \nL127: Handling Editor: Hugh Griffiths\nL128: \nL129: Sections\nL130: \nL131: CITE\nL132: \nL133: 【19†Tools】\nL134: \nL135: Share full-text access\nL136: \nL137: Please review our 【20†Terms and Conditions of Use†onlinelibrary.wiley.com】 and check box below to share full-text version of article.\nL138: \nL139: [Input]I have read and accept the Wiley Online Library Terms and Conditions of Use\nL140: \nL141: * * *\nL142: \nL143: Shareable Link\nL144: Use the link below to share a full-text version of this article with your friends and colleagues. 【21†Learn more.†onlinelibrary.wiley.com】\nL145: \nL146: [Button: Copy URL]\nL147: \nL148: Share a link\nL149: \nL150: Share on\nL151: ## ABSTRACT\nL152: Time difference of arrival (TDOA) estimation is a critical parameter estimation stage of TDOA localisation in wireless monitoring networks, and its result directly determines the positioning accuracy of the signal source. However, the existing TDOA estimation methods based on orthogonal matching pursuit (OMP) only consider the scenario of circular signal and are prone to the degradation of estimation performance due to atom misselection under small samples and low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR).\nL153: To address this issue, this paper proposes a loop matching pursuit multistation TDOA estimation algorithm based on noncircular signal. Firstly, by combining the noncircular characteristic of the radiation source signal, an extended data model is constructed by using multistation received data and their conjugate. Then, based on the time-domain sparsity of the time difference, an extended time-domain dictionary set is built by discretising the time grid.\nL154: In addition, the support set is optimised via atom loop deletion and addition to reconstruct the sparse coefficients. Finally, the TDOA estimation is obtained from the correspondence between the time difference value and the sparse coefficient. Furthermore, the Cramer–Rao bound (CRB) for TDOA estimation of a noncircular source is derived, thereby providing a quantitative theoretical lower bound for the estimation accuracy of the new algorithm.\nL155: The simulation experiment results verify the superiority of the proposed algorithm under small samples and low SNR.\nL156: ## 1 Introduction\nL157: \nL158: In wireless communication systems, positioning technology based on time difference of arrival (TDOA) has become an important research direction in the field of wireless positioning due to its advantages, such as strong real-time performance and high positioning accuracy [【22†1】-【23†5】]. This technique operates by calculating the time differences of signal arrival between multiple sensors, rather than absolute time. Therefore, the core problem of this technology is TDOA estimation.\nL159: However, in passive reconnaissance scenarios, in order to avoid being intercepted by a third party, the signal transmitter often intentionally suppresses the signal power, resulting in a low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of the received signal. Meanwhile, the monitoring targets often emit short burst signals, which makes it difficult to collect a large number of samples for positioning. A precise positioning result hinges on obtaining a robust and reliable TDOA estimation.\nL160: Therefore, the study of high-precision TDOA estimation methods under low SNR and a small number of samples carries a significant challenge.\nL161: An effective approach for improving parameter estimation accuracy under low SNR conditions involves leveraging prior information, particularly the characteristic information of the signal [【24†6】]. Noncircular characteristics are one of the most common properties in communication signals, such as BPSK, AM and MASK. The inclusion of noncircular information in parameter estimation facilitates further enhancement of estimation accuracy.\nL162: In addition, compressed sensing (CS) technology based on the signal sparse characteristic exhibits significant advantages in parameter estimation under a small number of samples [【25†7】-【26†10】]. Among this technology, the orthogonal matching pursuit (OMP) algorithm is widely used due to its stability and low complexity [【27†11】, 【28†12】]. However, its atom selection accuracy is relatively low under low SNR conditions.\nL163: To improve the performance of TDOA estimation under small samples and low SNR, we propose a loop matching pursuit multistation TDOA estimation algorithm based on noncircular signal (NC-LMP). By introducing the signal’s noncircular characteristic into the OMP reconstruction algorithm, an extended received signal model based on sparse representation is constructed.\nL164: Meanwhile, aiming at the OMP atom misselection problem, an optimised support set is updated through atom loop deletion-addition to reconstruct the sparse coefficients.\nL165: Specifically, the major contributions of this paper are as follows:\nL166: \nL167:   1. We establish a TDOA estimation model for noncircular signals. By constructing a joint sparse constraint term in the complex domain, we solve the problem of insufficient information utilisation under the assumption of a traditional circular signal.\nL168:   2. We propose an improved loop matching pursuit signal reconstruction algorithm. Our work introduces the atom backtracking filter mechanism into the traditional OMP algorithm, addressing the challenge that atoms could not be updated after selection.\nL169: \nL170:   3. This work derives the CRB for TDOA estimation of noncircular signals, providing a theoretical basis for system parameter design and algorithm performance evaluation. The superiority of the proposed algorithm is validated through simulation experiments.\nL171: The rest of this article is structured as follows: In Section 【29†3】, the multistation signal observation model and OMP algorithm are presented. In Section 【30†4】, the proposed NC-LMP algorithm is described in detail. The TDOA estimation CRB of a noncircular signal is derived and compared with the CRB of a circular signal in Section 【31†5】. Numerical simulation and detailed case study analysis are discussed in Section 【32†6】. Finally, the conclusion is given in Section 【33†7】.\nL172: ## 2 Related Work\nL173: ### 2.1 Traditional TDOA Estimation Methods\nL174: The traditional TDOA estimation methods can be roughly classified into three categories: correlation-based method, maximum likelihood method (ML) and subspace-based method. The correlation-based method [【34†13】, 【35†14】] is computationally efficient and straightforward to implement, but it is susceptible to false peaks under low SNR, leading to degraded estimation accuracy. To solve these problems, several improvements have been proposed: W. Liu et al. [【36†15】] and G. Liu et al.\nL175: [【37†16】] employed a prefiltering technique to preprocess signals for noise suppression. Yang et al. [【38†17】] introduced an enhanced TDOA estimation method that combines polynomial fitting and interpolation with the generalised cross-correlation approach, thereby improving time resolution. However, because these methods still fundamentally rely on correlation operations, their accuracy improvements remain inherently constrained under low SNR.\nL176: The ML method [【39†18】, 【40†19】] can theoretically achieve optimal performance. However, when constructing the likelihood function, it requires a large amount of observed data and the probability density function (PDF) of the received signal.\nL177: Subspace methods represented by multiple signal classification (MUSIC) [【41†20】] showcase super-resolution performance, but they suffer from significant performance degradation under low SNR or a small number of samples due to the inaccurate separation between the signal and noise subspace. Lu et al. [【42†21】] constructed weighting values using signal eigenvalues and noise power to weight the subspace and correct the estimated spectrum. Ma et al.\nL178: [【43†22】] proposed a method that utilises an exponential kernel correlation function (EKC) to suppress non-Gaussian impulsive noise. We should note that these traditional methods generally ignore the sparsity characteristics of time difference in the time domain and impose stringent requirements on both SNR and the number of samples. Therefore, CS is employed in this paper to address the TDOA estimation problem.\nL179: ### 2.2 CS Reconstruction Algorithm\nL180: Among the existing CS reconstruction algorithms, the convex optimisation algorithms achieve sparse reconstruction through $ ${l}_{0}$$ norm minimisation [【44†23】, 【45†24】]. Although theoretically optimal, they rely on linear programming for solutions, which results in high computational complexity and difficulty in meeting the real-time requirements in TDOA estimation. Greedy algorithms balance efficiency and accuracy through iterative approximation [【46†25】-【47†27】].\nL181: Among them, the OMP reconstruction algorithm features a straightforward principle and low computational complexity. However, because it approximates the true solution by gradually selecting atoms, once the wrong atom is selected, it will not be deleted, which affects the subsequent atom screening and the final reconstruction accuracy.\nL182: For this case, Leng et al. [【48†28】] introduced a backtracking filter mechanism based on the OMP algorithm and corrected the support set through amplification and reduction. Hu et al. [【49†29】] proposed a modified phase transform OMP algorithm (M-PHAT-OMP), which utilised the clustering characteristics of the M-PHAT-weighted algorithm to compute energy around the true value and found the maximum value of the energy through a sliding window.\nL183: However, because the secondary filter process was still carried out based on the initially selected atoms, the estimation accuracy remains fundamentally constrained. In this paper, a backtracking screening mechanism is incorporated into the OMP framework to mitigate the impact of initial atom misselection on reconstruction accuracy.\nL184: ### 2.3 Noncircular Prior Information\nL185: The above methods are all based on the circular signal model. In the real scenario, the radiation source signal often presents noncircular characteristics, which will lead to a decrease in estimation accuracy if ignored. There are numerous parameter estimation algorithms for noncircular signals, such as DOA estimation [【50†30】, 【51†31】], target localisation and tracking [【52†32】, 【53†33】] etc. However, the noncircular information in TDOA estimation has not been fully exploited. Only Wen et al.\nL186: [【54†34】] have explored the TDOA estimation problem of Gaussian noncircular complex signals, but their ML algorithm is limited to a small number of samples. Conceivably, if the noncircular information of the signal can be exploited in TDOA estimation, it is also expected to further increase the accuracy.\nL187: In summary, the noncircularity of signals has not yet been fully exploited in TDOA estimation. Meanwhile, the atom misselection problem existing in the OMP algorithm under low SNR has not been effectively solved, which constrains its accuracy, and this is exactly our breakthrough point.\nL188: ## 3 Signal Model and OMP Algorithm\nL189: ### 3.1 Signal Model\nL190: The schematic of TDOA positioning is shown in Figure 【55†1】. This system consists of one main station $ ${r}_{0}$$ and $ $M$$ auxiliary stations $ ${r}_{m}$$. The signals emitted by the radiation source propagate through free space and are received by the observation stations. The auxiliary observation stations transmit the data to the main observation station for processing.\nL191: Because the stations' positions are significantly different, the time at which they receive signals from the radiation source is generally different. A set of TDOAs is determined between the radiation source and the stations.\nL192: FIGURE 1\nL193: \nL194: 【19†Open in figure viewer】【56†PowerPoint】\nL195: \nL196: Schematic of TDOA positioning.\nL197: \nL198: Suppose the TDOA of the signals from the transmitting point to the main station and the auxiliary station is constant within the observation interval. Then, the signal model received by the main station and the auxiliary stations can be expressed as follows:\nL199: $$ $\\left\\{\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{r}_{0}(n)={a}_{0}s\\left(n-{\\tau }_{0}\\right)+{w}_{0}(n)\\\\ {r}_{m}(n)={a}_{m}s\\left(n-{\\tau }_{m}\\right)+{w}_{m}(n)\\end{array}\\right.,$$$ (1)\nL200: where $ $1\\le m\\le M$$ and $ $n=0,1,\\text{\\ldots },N-1$$. $ $N$$ is the number of discrete sampling points, and the sampling period $ ${T}_{s}$$ is assumed to be unit 1.\nL201: $ ${r}_{0}(n)$$ and $ ${r}_{m}(n)$$ are, respectively, complex observation signals of the main station and the auxiliary stations, $ ${a}_{0}$$ and $ ${a}_{m}$$ are amplitude attenuation coefficients, $ $s(n)$$ is the unknown definite radiation complex signal, $ ${\\tau }_{0}$$ and $ ${\\tau }_{m}$$ are the time delays of the source signal reaching the main station and the auxiliary stations, and are additive complex white Gaussian noise with power $ ${\\sigma }_{0}^{2}$$ and $ ${\\sigma }_{m}^{2}$$, and $ ${w}_{0}(n)$$ and $ ${w}_{m}(n)$$ are not correlated and are, respectively, not correlated with the source signal $ $s(n)$$.\nL202: Taking the main station’s received signal as the reference signal, Equation (【57†1】) can be written as follows:\nL203: \nL204: $$ $\\left\\{\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{r}_{0}(n)=s(n)+{w}_{0}(n)\\\\ {r}_{m}(n)={\\lambda }_{m}s\\left(n-{\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}\\right)+{w}_{m}(n)\\end{array}\\right.,$$$ (2)\nL205: where $ ${\\lambda }_{m}={a}_{m}/{a}_{0}$$ is the attenuation coefficient ratio, and $ ${\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}={\\tau }_{m}-{\\tau }_{0}$$ is the TDOA. In passive TDOA estimation, although the time delays $ ${\\tau }_{0}$$ and $ ${\\tau }_{m}$$ are unknown, we only need to obtain the TDOA $ ${\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}$$.\nL206: \nL207: Take $ ${s}_{m}(n)\\triangleq s\\left(n-{\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}\\right)$$ as the time-shift signal and rewrite Equation (【58†2】) in the vector form:\nL208: $$ $\\left\\{\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{\\boldsymbol{r}}_{0}=\\boldsymbol{s}+{\\boldsymbol{w}}_{0}\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}={\\lambda }_{m}{\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}+{\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m}\\end{array}\\right.,$$$ (3)\nL209: \nL210: where\nL211: $$ $\\left\\{\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{\\boldsymbol{r}}_{0}={\\left[{r}_{0}(0),{r}_{0}(1),{\\cdots},{r}_{0}(N-1)\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}={\\left[{r}_{m}(0),{r}_{m}(1),{\\cdots},{r}_{m}(N-1)\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}\\\\ \\boldsymbol{s}={[s(0),s(1),{\\cdots},s(N-1)]}^{\\mathrm{T}}\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}={\\left[s\\left(0-{\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}\\right),s\\left(1-{\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}\\right),{\\cdots},s\\left(N-1-{\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}\\right)\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{w}}_{0}={\\left[{w}_{0}(0),{w}_{0}(1),{\\cdots},{w}_{0}(N-1)\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m}={\\left[{w}_{m}(0),{w}_{m}(1),{\\cdots},{w}_{m}(N-1)\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}\\end{array}\\right..$$$ (4)\nL212: ### 3.2 OMP Algorithm\nL213: In order to make full use of the signal sparsity in the time domain, the CS method transforms the TDOA estimation problem into sparse coefficient recovery and exploits the reconstruction algorithm to restore the TDOA parameter.\nL214: The time-domain interval is divided into $ $L$$ equal parts for signal sparsification first; that is, the TDOA grid composed of $ $\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\tau }}=\\left\\{{\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{1},{\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{2},{\\cdots},{\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{L}\\right\\}$$ covers all possible TDOA values, and each value corresponds to an attenuation coefficient. To ensure sparsity, the number of potential TDOAs should be much greater than the actual number of TDOAs.\nL215: If the radiation signal is known, a waveform matching dictionary can usually be constructed by using the known signal and its time shift. In that case, the received signal admits a sparse representation under this waveform matching dictionary. However, in passive reconnaissance, it is hard to acquire the radiation signal waveform. Then, the reference signal can be shifted to construct the matching dictionary, and the auxiliary station signal also has a sparse representation under this matching dictionary.\nL216: Define an over-complete TDOA steering vector dictionary:\nL217: \nL218: $$ $\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }=\\left[\\boldsymbol{a}\\left({\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{1}\\right),\\boldsymbol{a}\\left({\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{2}\\right),{\\cdots},\\boldsymbol{a}\\left({\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{L}\\right)\\right]\\in {\\mathbb{C}}^{N\\times L},$$$ (5)\nL219: $$ $\\boldsymbol{a}\\left({\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{l}\\right)={\\left[{r}_{0}\\left(0-{\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{l}\\right),{r}_{0}\\left(1-{\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{l}\\right),{\\cdots},{r}_{0}\\left(N-1-{\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{l}\\right)\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}.$$$ (6)\nL220: \nL221: Suppose the true TDOAs $ ${\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}$$ are contained in the over-complete TDOA gird $ $\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\tau }}$$, then the sparse representation model of the signal received by observation station $ $m$$ in Equation (【59†3】) can be written as follows:\nL222: $$ ${\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}=\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }{\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}+{\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m},$$$ (7)\nL223: where $ ${\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}={\\left[{\\widetilde{\\lambda }}_{1},{\\widetilde{\\lambda }}_{2},{\\cdots},{\\widetilde{\\lambda }}_{L}\\right]}^{\\mathrm{T}}$$ is the sparse coefficient vector. $ ${\\widetilde{\\lambda }}_{l}$$ is nonzero only when $ ${\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m}={\\widetilde{\\tau }}_{l}$$, whereas the remaining elements are zero.\nL224: Hence, signal TDOA estimation is transformed into finding the nonzero positions of $ ${\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}$$ with $ ${\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}$$ and $ $\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }$$ known.\nL225: Candes et al. [【60†35】] have proved that the sparse reconstruction problem can be solved by calculating the minimum $ ${l}_{0}$$ norm, that is, $ ${\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}$$ will achieve complete reconstruction through $ ${l}_{0}$$ norm minimisation:\nL226: $$ $\\underset{{\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}}{\\min }{\\Vert {\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}\\Vert }_{0}\\mathrm{s}.\\mathrm{t}.{\\Vert {\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}-\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }{\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}\\Vert }_{2}\\le \\varepsilon ,$$$ (8)\nL227: \nL228: where $ $\\varepsilon $$ is the error threshold.\nL229: The OMP algorithm gradually selects the atoms that best match the signal from the atom dictionary to approximate the original signal. In iteration $ $k$$, find the index that best matches the current residual signal:\nL230: \nL231: $$ ${\\theta }^{[k]}=\\mathrm{arg}\\underset{j=1,2,{\\cdots},L}{\\max }\\vert \\left\\langle {\\boldsymbol{y}}^{[k-1]},{\\boldsymbol{\\phi }}_{j}\\right\\rangle \\vert ,$$$ (9)\nL232: where $ ${\\boldsymbol{y}}^{[k]}$$ is the residual in iteration $ $k$$, $ ${\\theta }^{[k]}$$ is the index found in iteration $ $k$$, $ ${\\boldsymbol{\\phi }}_{j}$$ is the $ $j$$ column of matrix $ $\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }$$.\nL233: \nL234: Add the best-matching atoms found and their indices to the atom set and the support set, respectively:\nL235: \nL236: $$ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Theta }}^{[k]}={\\boldsymbol{\\Theta }}^{[k-1]}\\cup \\left\\{{\\theta }^{[k]}\\right\\},$$$ (10)\nL237: $$ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }}^{[k]}={\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }}^{[k-1]}\\cup {\\boldsymbol{\\phi }}_{{\\theta }^{[k],}}$$$ (11)\nL238: \nL239: where $ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Theta }}^{[k]}$$ is the index set for $ $k$$ iterations, and $ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }}^{[k]}$$ (size is $ $4N\\times k$$) is the column set of the matrix $ $\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }$$ selected by index $ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Theta }}^{[k]}$$. Then, we calculate the sparse coefficient estimation in the current iteration using the LS method:\nL240: $$ ${\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}^{[k]}=\\mathrm{arg}\\underset{{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}^{[k]}}{\\min }\\Vert {\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}-{\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }}^{[k]}{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}^{[k]}\\Vert ={\\left({\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }}^{[k]}\\right)}^{{\\dagger}}{\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m},$$$ (12)\nL241: \nL242: where $ ${\\dagger}$$ represents the Moore–Penrose inverse. Next, the residual in iteration $ $k$$ is updated as follows:\nL243: $$ ${\\boldsymbol{y}}^{[k]}={\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}-{\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }}^{[k]}{\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}^{[k]}.$$$ (13)\nL244: \nL245: Iteration $ $k$$ is completed now. The results $ ${\\boldsymbol{y}}^{[k]}$$ and $ ${\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}^{[k]}$$ are output when the condition $ ${\\Vert {\\boldsymbol{y}}^{[k]}\\Vert }_{2}\\le \\varepsilon $$ is satisfied; otherwise, take $ $k=k+1$$ and the process proceeds to the next iteration.\nL246: After the iteration is completed, $ ${\\widetilde{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}_{m}$$ only has one nonzero term at $ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Theta }}^{[k]}$$, whose value is $ ${\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{\\lambda }}}^{[k]}$$ obtained in the last iteration, and the other elements are zero. Then, the TDOA value corresponding to the position $ ${\\boldsymbol{\\Theta }}^{[k]}$$ in the dictionary $ $\\boldsymbol{\\Phi }$$ is the TDOA to be estimated.\nL247: Following this method, $ $M$$ auxiliary stations yield $ $M$$ sets of TDOAs, which can then be used to locate the radiation source.\nL248: ## 4 Proposed Method\nL249: This section describes how signal noncircular prior information can be introduced into the TDOA estimation model while improving the OMP reconstruction algorithm. Specifically, we first concatenate and double the received signals from auxiliary stations to construct an extended sparse model. Subsequently, a waveform matching dictionary is generated by cyclically shifting the reference signal, under which the extended signal exhibits sparse representation.\nL250: This enables the establishment of a TDOA estimation model based on the reference signal’s waveform matching dictionary. The sparse coefficients are then reconstructed through an improved OMP algorithm to obtain TDOA estimation, with the entire process requiring no complete reconstruction of the original signal.\nL251: Based on the principle above, Figure 【61†2】 illustrates the comprehensive technical framework of the proposed algorithm.\nL252: \nL253: FIGURE 2\nL254: \nL255: 【19†Open in figure viewer】【62†PowerPoint】\nL256: \nL257: Comprehensive technical framework of the proposed algorithm.\nL258: ### 4.1 Noncircular Model Extension\nL259: \nL260: According to the time-domain characteristic of the signal with maximum noncircularity rate [【63†36】], the source signal $ $\\boldsymbol{s}$$ can be expressed as follows:\nL261: \nL262: $$ $\\boldsymbol{s}={\\boldsymbol{s}}_{0}{\\mathrm{e}}^{j\\varphi },$$$ (14)\nL263: where $ ${\\boldsymbol{s}}_{0}$$ is a real-valued signal, and $ $\\varphi $$ is the signal's initial phase. At present, many classical parameter estimation algorithms are predicated on the assumption of signals possessing the maximum noncircularity rate. Consequently, this paper specifically focuses on signals with maximum noncircularity rate.\nL264: Based on this time-domain characteristic, the conjugate $ ${\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}^{\\ast }$$ of the time-shift signal $ ${\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}$$ can be expressed as follows:\nL265: $$ ${\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}^{\\ast }={\\boldsymbol{s}}_{0}^{\\ast }{\\mathrm{e}}^{-j\\varphi }={\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}{\\mathrm{e}}^{-j2\\varphi }.$$$ (15)\nL266: \nL267: In TDOA estimation, to explore the signal’s noncircular characteristic, the auxiliary station’s signal is concatenated with its conjugate to construct an extended signal model:\nL268: $$ $\\begin{array}{ll}{\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m,\\text{NC}}& =\\left[\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m}^{\\ast }\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{@{}c@{}}{\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}{\\lambda }_{m}\\\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}^{\\ast }{\\lambda }_{m}^{\\ast }\\end{array}\\right]+\\left[\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m}\\\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m}^{\\ast }\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{@{}c@{}}{\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}\\\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m}{\\mathrm{e}}^{-\\mathrm{j}2\\varphi }\\end{array}\\right]{\\lambda }_{m}+\\left[\\begin{array}{@{}l@{}}{\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m}\\\\\\ {\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m}^{\\ast }\\end{array}\\right]\\\\ & \\triangleq {\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m,\\text{NC}}\\left({\\Delta }{\\tau }_{m},\\varphi \\right){\\lambda }_{m}+{\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m,\\text{NC}}\\end{array},$$$ (16)\nL269: where the extended signal vector $ ${\\boldsymbol{r}}_{m,\\text{NC}}$$, extended steering vector $ ${\\boldsymbol{s}}_{m,\\text{NC}}$$ and extended noise vector $ ${\\boldsymbol{w}}_{m,\\text{NC}}$$ are defined as follows:\n"
